          },
          "mechanism": "Chronic arrhythmia can lead to or contribute to congestive heart failure development; congestive heart failure and arrhythmia represent related cardiac dysfunction.",
          "justification": "Excluded to avoid adjusting for descendants of the outcome.",
          "role": "descendant_of_outcome",
          "proxy_for": null,
          "contestable": false
        },
        {
          "variable": {
            "kind": "variable",
            "key": "m2:Q5.42",
            "quoted_wording": "sha256:c907cdc050458359f8763d8241f72993b217795055f0adf6e5489a718754f223"
          },
          "mechanism": "Valvular disease is part of the same cardiovascular disease process as arrhythmia and can result from it.",
          "justification": "Excluded to avoid adjusting for descendants of the outcome.",
          "role": "descendant_of_outcome",
          "proxy_for": null,
          "contestable": false
        },
        {
          "variable": {
            "kind": "variable",
            "key": "m2:Q5.60",
            "quoted_wording": "sha256:1c79cf22c5e0d001c0dcfc604a6b91ed634a23f43a2d0ec4df3ce0ae84f7110f"
          },
          "mechanism": "Opioids suppress respiration and worsen sleep apnea through the mediator pathway: opioids → worse apnea → arrhythmia. Sleep apnea itself causes arrhythmia through oxygen desaturation.",
          "justification": "Excluded to preserve the indirect pathway through respiratory depression if it exists; including it would control away a potential mechanism of opioid-induced arrhythmia.",
          "role": "mediator",
          "proxy_for": null,
          "contestable": false
        }
      ],
